# External revisions this harness is verified against.
#
# A pin is a SHA, never a branch name. A branch tip moves, so a green run
# against one is not attributable to anything: the next run tests different
# code and nobody can say which change turned it red. CI checks out exactly
# these revisions, so a bump is a reviewable diff carrying its own evidence
# rather than silent drift.
#
# Bumping a pin is deliberate work: move the checkout (make checkout-oss), run
# BOTH runtimes (make test && make test-compose), then commit the new SHA. Do
# not bump it to make a red run green without understanding what changed.
#
# In practice the running is already done for you. oss-crosscheck.yml runs
# that exact pair nightly, and when it comes back green against a newer oss,
# oss-pin-bump.yml prepares the bump on chore/bump-oss-pin and files an issue
# linking the run that proved it. What is left is the part that wanted a human
# anyway: reading what changed in oss before merging it.
#
# Pin a commit that is ON the branch it claims to track — never a PR-branch
# SHA. A rebase or squash merge rewrites it, so such a pin is stale the instant
# the pull request lands while still resolving through the merged PR's ref,
# which is the worst of both: wrong and quiet.
#
# The reverse pin (provin.oss's E2E_REF, naming a revision of THIS repo) is the
# same hazard pointed the other way: that gate can go on testing a superseded
# harness, silently and greenly, and it did — the bump was missed the same day
# the gate was built. It now has an equivalent of this file to read
# (provin.oss's own pins.env) and an equivalent job to move it
# (provin.oss's e2e-pin-bump.yml), which reads THIS repository's nightly run to
# decide when a bump is safe.
#
# Neither of those jobs reddens a build to ask for a bump. One that did lived
# here until 2026-08-05, and the eight consecutive red nights it produced are
# why the rule is now explicit: a bookkeeping obligation is filed as work, not
# raised as a failure. Red is reserved for something being broken.
#
# Tracking the counterpart's HEAD is a separate, NON-gating job
# (.github/workflows/oss-crosscheck.yml) — the same split provin.oss's own
# spec-crosscheck.yml uses for the dPLaaX spec: the in-tree pin is what the
# regular gate runs against, and watching HEAD is an early signal only.
#
# Note that the gate this pin serves is only half the pairing. It catches
# "an e2e change broke against known-good oss". The other direction — "an oss
# change broke the scenarios" — cannot be gated from here at all, because
# nothing in this repository runs when oss changes; that job lives in
# provin.oss and pins a revision of THIS repo.

# github.com/provin-line/oss — the product under test.
#
# NOTE (2026-08-05, updated same day): when this pin landed it named a
# then-unmerged PR-branch SHA, knowingly bending the header's rule because the
# paper's measurements were taken against exactly this revision. oss PR #37
# has since been merged WITH A MERGE COMMIT (db50ad72) precisely so this
# revision became an ancestor of main — the rule is satisfied again, no
# re-pin is owed, and the earlier instruction to re-pin to the merge result
# is obsolete. The next move of this line is an ordinary forward bump.
OSS_REF=b9d2f5a1442d356dacb10af48ff95be45a875971
